G="An early Father on the Eucharist"
E=[(G,217,"The Church has known this all along. Sacred Tradition","The Church has known this all along. Early in the second century, St. Ignatius of Antioch, bishop and martyr, wrote to the Christians of Ephesus as he was being taken to Rome to die. He urged them to stay united, \"breaking one and the same bread, which is the medicine of immortality, and the antidote to prevent us from dying, but [which causes] that we should live for ever in Jesus Christ.\"<sup>1</sup> Sacred Tradition"),
(G,"Further Study","- **St. Augustine (354-430), Bishop of Hippo","- **St. Ignatius of Antioch (died early 2nd century), Bishop of Antioch and martyr** - His letters, written on the road to his martyrdom in Rome, are among the earliest witnesses to the Eucharist as the bread that binds the Church to Christ and to one another\n- **St. Augustine (354-430), Bishop of Hippo"),
(G,"Notes","q. 73-83 on the Eucharist as sacrament of unity and charity\n","q. 73-83 on the Eucharist as sacrament of unity and charity\n\n### Notes\n\n\\footnotesize\n\n1. St. Ignatius of Antioch, *Letter to the Ephesians* 20, in *Ante-Nicene Fathers*, vol. 1.\n\n\\normalsize\n"),
]
